WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.290
- [Narrator] Is this
food making you hungry?

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:04.320
Well, don't try to take a bite

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.683
because these delicious looking
foods are actually fake.

00:00:08.896 --> 00:00:11.729
(whimsical music)

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.480
TV shows and movies will
try to use real foods

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.240
on screen when possible,

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.740
but there a number of
reasons why food props

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:22.100
might be used instead.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.590
For example, if ice cream is used,

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.570
they don't want it to melt between takes,

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:29.570
or if you need a lot of food
in the background of a shot.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:31.750
Companies like Independent Studio Services

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.960
and Display Fake Foods
offer pre-made food props

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.550
that can be ordered in bulk.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.246
But often times, movies
need items specially made.

00:00:39.246 --> 00:00:42.460
That's when they seek
out a fake food artist,

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.110
like Lisa Friedman.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.070
- For people who need something specific,

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:47.520
that's why the reach out to me.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:48.353
I'm an artist.

00:00:48.353 --> 00:00:52.330
I went to school for art and
I also love to cook and bake.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:55.820
There's not a lot of us
out there that do this.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:57.370
- [Narrator] Brenda Chapman also makes

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:58.890
fake foods in Oklahoma.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:00:59.746
- I just kind of figure it out.

00:00:59.746 --> 00:01:02.950
I've had no formal training,
didn't go to college.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:04.317
I started this just so I
could be a stay-at-home mom

00:01:04.317 --> 00:01:05.460
with my kids.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.090
- [Narrator] Both women work
out of studios in their homes.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:10.440
They can recreate pretty much anything.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.930
Much of their day-to-day business

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:14.260
is in restaurant displays and food shows.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:15.900
But prop masters will contact them

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.310
if they need food items for movies.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:22.010
- In the last 20 years, I've
done almost 3 million dollars

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:24.220
worth of fake food business.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.810
- [Narrator] Brenda has
had her work featured

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:27.480
in a number of productions.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.100
For Glee, she made some ice
cream for this diner scene.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.520
- [Brenda] In their diner scene,

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.640
they wanted milk shakes
and hot fudge sundaes

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:40.220
and banana splits that were
new, half-eaten, quarter-eaten

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.550
so that they could switch
them out during the takes.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:45.030
- [Narrator] She says
you don't always know

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.460
where your food will end up.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.020
Like when some of her items
popped up in the Muppets.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.160
- When Miss Piggy eats my doughnuts,

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.048
I didn't realize they
had bought my doughnuts.

00:01:53.048 --> 00:01:54.157
(gluttonous munching)

00:01:54.157 --> 00:01:55.510
- Pardon moi, Mademoiselle Cochonne?

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:57.200
- Can't you see I'm busy!

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.740
- [Receptionist] Of course.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:01:59.730
- [Narrator] And sometimes your food

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:01.750
doesn't even make the final cut.

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:04.037
- Thor, the movie, actually bought like

00:02:04.037 --> 00:02:05.810
$500 worth of doughnuts,

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.060
and they had a building
that said Donut Shop

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:09.690
or Donut Land, they never went inside,

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:10.830
so I didn't get to see my doughnuts.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.223
I was very sad. (laughs)

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:16.110
- [Narrator] Here's a
creamsicle Lisa Friedman made

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.340
that was featured in a scene from

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.730
Kevin (Probably) Saves the World.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:21.120
- The coldest thing they have.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.450
- Oh, thank you.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:24.090
- [Lisa] I guess, his eye was swollen,

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:25.160
he got hit in it.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.610
- [Narrator] While the details
may vary based on the artist,

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.930
the creation process is pretty standard.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:32.100
We stopped by Lisa
Friedman's home in New York

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.650
to see how she makes her fake foods.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.200
After the order is submitted,

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.560
typically the customer will
send her a real version

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.160
of they want duplicated.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.310
Then she will produce
a mold out of the item

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:44.960
to get the exact size and shape.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.450
- We try to mold it close to the color,

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.443
so that we're not starting
with a blank white canvas.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:56.470
- [Narrator] Typically fake foods

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:58.510
are made with rubber or foam.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:01.700
She pours the material into
the mold and lets it set.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.090
Foam rises like actual dough,

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.343
so she needs to prevent
it from spilling out.

00:03:06.343 --> 00:03:07.990
- [Linda] It's like I'm
baking a cake, right?

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:09.223
I'm baking my bread.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.393
- [Narrator] Then she sands
the excess pieces down.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:21.740
Once the item is dry,

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.530
it's painted and detailed
to look like real food.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.600
- With my background in painting,

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:31.373
I can color it to be
as realistic as it is.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.270
- You just kind of have to look at things

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.460
a little differently, um,

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.840
and think, okay, it's not made for this

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:41.970
but it does look like this.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.240
We use a lot of Styrofoams,

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:48.840
a lot of stuff from the local
hardware store, you know,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:53.420
caulking, and dry wall
patching, and sheet rock mud.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.540
- [Narrator] To replicate
granola and ground beef,

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.600
Lisa uses crushed cork board.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.030
- Cork is kind of breaks up like granola,

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:03.550
so we took some cork boards and
we started breaking it down.

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.930
- [Narrator] Sometimes real food is used.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:09.670
Like covering actual
popcorn, cereal, or candy

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:11.660
in resin to preserve it.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:13.584
It's often hard to tell
the finished product

00:04:13.584 --> 00:04:15.130
from the original.

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:16.330
- I don't do this for the money.

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:20.030
It's more for the accolades,

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:23.860
when my customers write, oh,
I love it, it came out great.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:24.693
- [Narrator] And while these items

00:04:24.693 --> 00:04:26.410
might make your mouth water,

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:28.213
they're only a feast for your eyes.

00:04:29.062 --> 00:04:31.895
(whimsical music)

